
Thanks for joining the call. Please find below the summary of our discussion and action items.
Discussion Summary
Internal Accounts go-live is currently planned for 31 October 2026.
The discussion focused on understanding the impact to RDH and its partner systems to support the subAccountType attribute required by the AMS team.
Impact to RDH if subAccountType is added to the RDH response/schema:
RDH code changes would be required to support the new attribute.
A schema migration/data update would also be required to align the existing production data with the updated schema.
The required code is currently available in UAT as part of another planned release; the feasibility of taking up the required changes separately as a mid-release change needs to be assessed.
OpsUI: Dependency identified, as changes would be required to support the new attribute.
MRD: AMS team to connect with MRD directly to assess the impact/dependency.
GAE: As a downstream consumer of RDH data, GAE impact needs to be assessed. In addition to supporting the new attribute, any change to the RDH topic/schema may require corresponding changes on the GAE side, including topic-level changes.
Alternative approach – Static Data:
An alternative approach discussed was for the AMS team to continue using static data instead of consuming the new attribute through RDH.
This approach is already being used in production, where the required account-class data was provided and loaded as static data on Day 0.
The planned RDH API integration is part of a future major release and is not currently available in production.
The preferred approach from the RDH perspective is therefore to explore whether the existing static-data approach can be continued for the Internal Accounts release, which would avoid RDH code/schema changes and associated partner dependencies.
Action Items
